dren's Institute for Genomic Medicine, Howard Hughes Medical Institute, University of California, La Jolla, San Diego, CA, USA.\n-   24 Departments of Pediatric Neurology, Department of Child Neurology, Karadeniz Technical University Medical Faculty, Retired Lecturer, Trabzon, Turkey. mjgsonmez@yahoo.com.\n-   25 Yuksek Ihtisas University, Faculty of Medicine, Ankara, Turkey. mjgsonmez@yahoo.com.\n-   26 , Aziziye Mah. Cinnah Cad. 102/3, Cankaya, Ankara, Türkiye. mjgsonmez@yahoo.com.\n\n-   PMID: **38622473**\n-   PMCID: [PMC11489189](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11489189/)\n-   DOI: [10.1007/s12311-024-01690-1](https://doi.org/10.1007/s12311-024-01690-1)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\nFull text links Cite\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat AbstractPubMedPMID\n\n## Abstract\n\nPontocerebellar hypoplasia (PCH) is a heterogeneous group of neurodegenerative disorders characterized by hypoplasia and degeneration of the cerebellum and pons. We aimed to identify the clinical, laboratory, and imaging findings of the patients with diagnosed PCH with confirmed genetic analysis. We collected available clinical data, laboratory, and imaging findings in our retrospective multicenter national study of 64 patients with PCH in Turkey. The genetic analysis included the whole-exome sequencing (WES), targeted next-generation sequencing (NGS), or single gene analysis. Sixty-four patients with PCH were 28 female (43.8%) and 36 (56.3%) male. The patients revealed homozygous mutation in 89.1%, consanguinity in 79.7%, pregnancy at term in 85.2%, microcephaly in 91.3%, psychomotor retardation in 98.4%, abnormal neurological findings in 100%, seizure in 63.8%, normal biochemistry and metabolic investigations in 92.2%, and dysmorphic findings in 51.2%. The missense mutation was found to be the most common variant type in all patients with PCH. It was detected as CLP1 (n = 17) was the most common PCH related gene. The homozygous missense variant c.419G > A (p.Arg140His) was identified in all patients with CLP1. Moreover, all patients showed the same homozygous missense variant c.919G > T (p.A307S) in TSEN54 group (n = 6). In Turkey, CLP1 was identified as the most common causative gene with the identical variant c.419G > A; p.Arg140His. The current study supports that genotype data on PCH leads to phenotypic variability over a wide phenotypic spectrum.\n\n**Keywords:** CLP1; Genotype; Phenotype; Pontocerebellar Hypoplasia.\n\n© 2024. The Author(s).\n\n[PubMed Disclaimer](/disclaimer/)\n\n## Conflict of interest statement\n\nThe authors declare no competing interests.\n\n## Figures\n\n [![Fig. 1](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/73b9/11489189/7cd90687a9bb/12311_2024_1690_Fig1_HTML.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/73b9/11489189/f69f328b76be/12311_2024_1690_Fig1_HTML.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFig. 1\n\n**\n\nDistribution of PCH genes\n\n**\n\nFig. 1\n\n**\n\nDistribution of PCH genes\n\n**Fig. 1**\n\nDistribution of PCH genes\n\n [![Fig. 2](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/73b9/11489189/ae20c640f94d/12311_2024_1690_Fig2_HTML.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/73b9/11489189/32e4e3a62900/12311_2024_1690_Fig2_HTML.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFig. 2\n\n**\n\nVariant type in PCH\n\n**\n\nFig. 2\n\n**\n\nVariant type in PCH\n\n**Fig. 2**\n\nVariant type in PCH\n\n [![Fig. 3](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/73b9/11489189/599906b6a15a/12311_2024_1690_Fig3_HTML.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/73b9/11489189/279dc177d51c/12311_2024_1690_Fig3_HTML.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFig. 3\n\n**\n\n(a-d) Typical radiological findings shown—(a,…\n\n**\n\nFig. 3\n\n**\n\n(a-d) Typical radiological findings shown—(a, b) dragonfly pattern (comparatively preserved vermis and atrophic…\n\n**Fig. 3**\n\n(a-d) Typical radiological findings shown—(a, b) dragonfly pattern (comparatively preserved vermis and atrophic cerebellar hemispheres) in one patient with TSEN54 gene. (c, d) brainstem appearance of figure of 8 on the brain MRI of one patient with AMPD2 gene\n\n[See this image and copyright information in PMC](#)\n\n## References\n\n1.  1.  van Dijk T, Baas F, Barth PG, Poll-The BT. What’s new in pontocerebellar hypoplasia? An update on genes and subtypes. Orphanet J Rare Dis. 2018;13(1):92. - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1186/s13023-018-0826-2) - [PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6003036/) - [PubMed](/29903031/)\n2.  1.  Nuovo S, Micalizzi A, Romaniello R, Arrigoni F, Ginevrino M, Casella A, et al. Refining the mutational spectrum and gene-phenotype correlates in pontocerebellar hypoplasia: results of a multicentric study. J Med Genet. 2022;59(4):399–409. - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1136/jmedgenet-2020-107497) - [PubMed](/34085948/)\n3.  1.  Namavar Y, Barth PG, Kasher PR, van Ruissen F, Brockmann K, Bernert G, et al. Clinical, neuroradiological and genetic findings in pontocerebellar hypoplasia. Brain. 2011;134(Pt 1):143–56. - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1093/brain/awq287) - [PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9136852/) - [PubMed](/20952379/)\n4.  1.  Brun R. Zur Kenntnis der Bildungsfehler des Kleinhirns. I. Über Aplasie und Hypoplasie des Neozerebellums. Schweiz Arch Neurol Psychiat. 1917;1:61–123.\n5.  1.  Barth PG, Vrensen GF, Uylings HB, Oorthuys JW, Stam FC. Inherited syndrome of microcephaly, dyskinesia and pontocerebellar hypoplasia: a systemic atrophy with early onset. J Neurol Sci. 1990;97(1):25–42. - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-510x\\(90\\)90096-6) - [PubMed](/2370559/)\n\nShow all 52 references\n\n## Publication types\n\n-   Multicenter Study\n    \n    Actions\n    \n    -   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Multicenter+Study%22%5Bpt%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n    -   [Search in MeSH](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh?term=Multicenter+Study)\n    -   [Add to Search](#)\n    \n\n## MeSH terms\n\n-   Adolescent\n    \n    Actions\n    \n    -   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Adolescent%22%5BMeSH%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n    -   [Search in MeSH](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.g